<resources>
    <!--
        Settings search. The index in SettingsSearch.kt reads the row titles and
        subtitles straight from the screens that draw them, so almost nothing
        belongs here. A key lands in this file only when the screen has no
        resource of its own to point at, or when it is a word the search matches
        on but no screen draws.
    -->

    <!--
        The Languages row on the settings home names the languages that are
        turned on, so it has no fixed subtitle for the index to reuse. This is
        what a search result says about that screen instead.
    -->
    <string name="search_languages_subtitle">Add, delete and reorder your typing languages</string>

    <!--
        Words that a search ignores, because they are in too many settings to
        narrow anything down. Space separated. A query made only of these words
        keeps them, so that a search for "the" still answers.
        Translators: give the equivalent short function words of your language,
        not a translation of these ones.
    -->
    <string name="search_stop_words">a an and are as at be by for from in is it me my of on or the to with you your</string>

    <!--
        Groups of words that mean the same thing when you look for a setting.
        Type any word of a group and the search also looks for the others, at a
        lower score than the word you typed.

        The settings screens follow one glossary (config/i18n/STRINGS.md): they
        say "press and hold", never "long press", and "glide typing", never
        "swipe typing". These groups are the other side of that: the words the
        screens never use, joined to the word they do.

        Each item is one group, and the words in it are separated by spaces.
        Order does not matter and a word can be in more than one group.

        Translators: these are search words, not sentences. Give the words a
        person types in your language to look for the same setting, including
        the common misspellings and the alternative spellings. The list does not
        have to be the same length as the English one, and a group with no
        equivalent in your language can hold a single word.
    -->
